    "body": "Listen before you answer. People often use the same words to mean very different things, so one good question can save ten minutes of talking past each other. Once the claim is clear, give one reason at a time and let the other person respond.\n\nFirst Peter 3:15–16 connects giving a reason for our hope with gentleness and a clear conscience. Cleverness is not the goal. Truthful, fair, Christlike witness is.",
    "points": [
      "Ask, “What do you mean by that?” when the claim is vague.",
      "Ask why the person believes it before assuming you know their reason.",
      "Answer the strongest version of the objection you can fairly state.",
      "Do not claim more than your argument gives you.",
      "If you need to check something, say so and come back with a source.",
      "Keep the tone consistent with the faith you are defending."
    ],
    "sourceLabel": "Further reading: OCCA Foundational Apologetics",
    "sourceUrl": "https://theocca.org/foundational-apologetics/",
